\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Construction of the Magic Gem and its $D_4$-invariance. Top row: (a)~the Lo Shu square $S$; (b)~the centred cells $(x_{ij},y_{ij})$ with the heights $z_{ij}=s_{ij}-5$; (c)~the nine points $\mathbf  p_{ij}$ over the grid, labelled by the entries $s_{ij}$; (d)~their convex hull, the gem of \Cref  {ex:loshu}, with $f$-vector $(8,16,10)$; the centre point $(0,0,0)$ is the only interior point. Rows two and three (e--l): the eight images of the Lo Shu under $D_4$---the identity $e$, the rotations $r,r^2,r^3$ by $90^\circ ,180^\circ ,270^\circ $, the left--right and top--bottom reflections $h,v$, and the two diagonal reflections (transpose $t$ and anti-transpose $tr^2$)---each printed row by row beneath its gem. All eight gems are drawn from the same viewpoint; by \Cref  {prop:symmetry_invariance} they are the images of one another under the isometries $\hat  g$, so they are congruent, and all have $f$-vector $(8,16,10)$ and volume $18$.}}{6}{figure.24}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:construction}{{1}{6}{Construction of the Magic Gem and its $D_4$-invariance. Top row: (a)~the Lo Shu square $S$; (b)~the centred cells $(x_{ij},y_{ij})$ with the heights $z_{ij}=s_{ij}-5$; (c)~the nine points $\mathbf p_{ij}$ over the grid, labelled by the entries $s_{ij}$; (d)~their convex hull, the gem of \Cref {ex:loshu}, with $f$-vector $(8,16,10)$; the centre point $(0,0,0)$ is the only interior point. Rows two and three (e--l): the eight images of the Lo Shu under $D_4$---the identity $e$, the rotations $r,r^2,r^3$ by $90^\circ ,180^\circ ,270^\circ $, the left--right and top--bottom reflections $h,v$, and the two diagonal reflections (transpose $t$ and anti-transpose $tr^2$)---each printed row by row beneath its gem. All eight gems are drawn from the same viewpoint; by \Cref {prop:symmetry_invariance} they are the images of one another under the isometries $\hat g$, so they are congruent, and all have $f$-vector $(8,16,10)$ and volume $18$}{figure.24}{}}
