\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The Siamese gem of order $n=9$ after the affine reduction of \Cref  {prop:siamese-12}. (a)~The $81$ cells in the $(x,y)$-plane coloured by the layer value $\zeta \in \{+10,+1,0,-1,-10\}$, with the five lattice polygons $T,U,M,D,B$ outlined and the twelve vertex cells $v_1,\dots  ,v_{12}$ circled. (b)~The reduced polytope $\Phi (\mathcal  G(S))=\operatorname  {conv}\{(x,y,\zeta )\}$ with its $12$ vertices and $16$ facets ($12$ triangles, $4$ quadrilaterals); the shapes of the layers do not depend on $n$ except through scale, which is why the combinatorial type is constant for all odd $n\ge 5$.}}{21}{figure.118}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:siamese-layers}{{4}{21}{The Siamese gem of order $n=9$ after the affine reduction of \Cref {prop:siamese-12}. (a)~The $81$ cells in the $(x,y)$-plane coloured by the layer value $\zeta \in \{+10,+1,0,-1,-10\}$, with the five lattice polygons $T,U,M,D,B$ outlined and the twelve vertex cells $v_1,\dots ,v_{12}$ circled. (b)~The reduced polytope $\Phi (\mathcal G(S))=\operatorname {conv}\{(x,y,\zeta )\}$ with its $12$ vertices and $16$ facets ($12$ triangles, $4$ quadrilaterals); the shapes of the layers do not depend on $n$ except through scale, which is why the combinatorial type is constant for all odd $n\ge 5$}{figure.118}{}}
